# M9's own record=/recordmax= fixture -- recordmax was raised to 150 by
# the pre-launch review pass (was 4096) specifically to ALSO exercise the
# S2_GETSPECIALSTATS overflow-count path in the same sub-test, without a
# new unit or a second record=-flushing file: 24 (pcap file header) + the
# 3 known writes' own records (40+40+42=122 bytes) totals exactly 146,
# so recordmax=150 still fits all 3 (verify_record.py's exact-3-record,
# byte-compared host-side assertion is UNCHANGED) while a deliberately
# added 4th write (>=31 bytes needed for even the smallest possible
# record) reliably overflows it. Kept in the SAME unit/file rather than
# a second dedicated one on purpose: M13's own investigation root-caused
# a real Copperline/AROS floppy write-back corruption bug when ANY dos.library
# read happens after a record=-flushed file's own close in the same
# session -- the established, now-proven-safe invariant is "the record
# sub-test is the LAST DOS I/O of the whole session," which a second,
# separate record=-flushing unit would violate no matter which order the
# two ran in.
record=S:sana2loop/recorded.pcap
recordmax=150
